## 3. Use Case Diagram

### Description
A Use Case Diagram shows the **functional requirements** of the system from the perspective
of external actors. It answers: *"Who uses the system and what can they do with it?"*

**Actors:**
- **User** — provides a media file path via CLI and receives analysis results.
- **ElevenLabs API** — external service involved in the speech-to-text use case.
- **FFprobe** — system-level tool invoked for audio/video analysis.

**Use Cases:**

| Use Case | Description |
|---|---|
| UC1: Provide File Path | User passes a media file path as a CLI argument |
| UC2: Validate File | System checks the file exists and is a real file |
| UC3: Detect File Type | System identifies IMAGE, AUDIO, VIDEO, or UNKNOWN |
| UC4: Analyze Image | Extract dimensions, format, color mode, DPI, EXIF data |
| UC5: Analyze Audio | Extract codec, channels, sampling rate, bit rate, duration |
| UC6: Analyze Video | Extract resolution, FPS, bit rate, codec + embedded audio info |
| UC7: Enhance Image | Apply brightness/contrast/color/sharpness enhancements |
| UC8: Transcribe Speech | Upload audio to ElevenLabs and receive text transcription |
| UC9: Generate Report | Compile metadata and save as reports/report.json |

**Relationships:**
- UC4, UC5, UC6 each `<<include>>` UC9 (every analysis generates a report)
- UC7 `<<extends>>` UC4 (image enhancement is an optional add-on to image analysis)
- UC8 `<<extends>>` UC5 (transcription is an optional add-on to audio analysis)
- UC6 `<<include>>` UC5 (video analysis always extracts the embedded audio track too)

